1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
Imagine floating hundreds of kilometers above Earth.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:06,240
Below you, an entire planet.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:09,680
Around you, nothing but the darkness of space.

4
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:13,040
You're wearing a spacesuit designed to protect you from one of the

5
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,460
most hostile environments imaginable.

6
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:16,740
And suddenly,

7
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:19,620
you feel water touching the back of your head.

8
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:21,820
At first, it seems harmless.

9
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:23,200
Maybe it's sweat.

10
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,160
Maybe your drinking bag is leaking.

11
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:28,080
But then, the water begins to spread.

12
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:31,940
It reaches your ears, your eyes, your nose,

13
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,860
and within minutes, you can barely see.

14
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:37,940
You try to contact the people who are supposed to help you,

15
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:40,340
but your communication system is failing,

16
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,780
and you're still outside the spacecraft.

17
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:45,480
This isn't a science fiction movie.

18
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:51,000
On July 16th, 2013, an astronaut faced a terrifying emergency aboard

19
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:52,980
the International Space Station.

20
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:57,460
His name was Luca Parmitano, and he was about to discover that you

21
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,680
don't need an ocean to drown.

22
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:01,900
This is File Two,

23
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:03,620
The Unknown File.

24
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:08,480
Our story begins more than 400 kilometers above the surface of Earth.

25
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:11,840
The International Space Station was orbiting our planet,

26
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,720
carrying astronauts from different countries on an extraordinary scientific

27
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:16,520
mission.

28
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:21,220
Among them was Luca Parmitano, an Italian astronaut representing the

29
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:22,920
European Space Agency.

30
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,540
Before becoming an astronaut, Luca had served as a military test pilot.

31
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:32,200
He was highly trained, experienced, and familiar with dangerous situations,

32
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:36,480
but nothing could have fully prepared him for what was about to happen.

33
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:41,040
That day, Luca and NASA astronaut Chris Cassidy were scheduled to

34
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,560
perform a spacewalk.

35
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:45,620
The mission involved working outside the space station,

36
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:49,520
installing and routing equipment, and completing maintenance tasks.

37
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:53,800
Spacewalks are among the most challenging activities astronauts perform.

38
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:57,300
Outside the station, there is no breathable atmosphere.

39
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,780
The temperature environment can be extreme,

40
00:01:59,820 --> 00:02:02,940
and every movement must be carefully controlled.

41
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,440
A spacesuit isn't simply clothing.

42
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:08,820
It's a small, self-contained life support system.

43
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,720
It supplies oxygen, maintains pressure,

44
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:16,300
removes carbon dioxide, and helps regulate an astronaut's body temperature.

45
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:20,540
Even something that seems minor on Earth can become extremely dangerous

46
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:25,140
in space. But as Luca began his work, everything initially appeared

47
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:28,520
normal. He moved along the exterior of the station,

48
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:31,920
connected cables, and continued his assigned tasks.

49
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:34,080
He was making excellent progress.

50
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,720
The astronauts were even running ahead of schedule.

51
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:40,520
Then, approximately 44 minutes into the spacewalk,

52
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,780
Luca reported something unusual.

53
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:45,860
He could feel water at the back of his head,

54
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:47,420
inside his helmet.

55
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:51,000
At first, nobody understood where it was coming from.

56
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,640
One possibility was his drinking bag.

57
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:57,960
Astronauts carry water so they can stay hydrated during long spacewalks.

58
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,160
Maybe a small amount had leaked.

59
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:02,420
Another possibility was sweat.

60
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:05,600
Neither explanation seemed completely convincing.

61
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,200
The water felt unusually cold.

62
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:11,680
And more importantly, it appeared to be increasing.

63
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:16,280
Luca continued communicating with Mission Control as the crew investigated.

64
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:20,700
His fellow astronaut, Chris Cassidy, moved closer to inspect the helmet.

65
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:24,220
But the situation was gradually becoming more serious.

66
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:27,020
Water was collecting around Luca's head.

67
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:30,780
And in weightlessness, liquids behave very differently from how we're

68
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,380
used to seeing them on Earth.

69
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:36,620
On Earth, spilled water normally falls downward because of gravity.

70
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:41,340
But inside a spacesuit in orbit, there is no familiar downward direction.

71
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,100
Water can cling to surfaces, spread across fabric,

72
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:47,000
and form floating masses.

73
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:50,580
And once water begins collecting inside a sealed helmet,

74
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:53,280
removing it becomes incredibly difficult.

75
00:03:53,660 --> 00:03:55,100
Think about that for a moment.

76
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:58,920
You're wearing a helmet that you cannot safely remove outside the

77
00:03:58,960 --> 00:03:59,740
spacecraft.

78
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:04,700
You're surrounded by the vacuum of space and water is slowly spreading

79
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:06,020
around your face.

80
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:09,660
You can't simply take the helmet off, you can't reach inside with

81
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:13,400
your hand, and you can't step outside for some fresh air.

82
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,380
As the water continued accumulating, mission control decided to end

83
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:18,920
the spacewalk early.

84
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:21,660
Luca was instructed to return to the airlock.

85
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:23,700
That should have been the solution.

86
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:27,080
But getting back was about to become a nightmare.

87
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,240
As Luca started moving toward the station's airlock,

88
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:32,520
the water shifted inside his helmet.

89
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,560
It began covering his eyes.

90
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:37,200
His vision became increasingly blurred.

91
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:41,800
Then, water reached his ears and the communication system began experiencing

92
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,600
problems. He could hear the voices of his fellow astronaut in mission

93
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:48,220
control, but increasingly faintly.

94
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,720
They also had difficulty hearing him.

95
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:54,860
Now imagine trying to navigate the exterior of a spacecraft while

96
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:56,600
your vision is disappearing.

97
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:59,300
You cannot simply walk back to the entrance.

98
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:03,160
Every movement involves handholds, carefully planned routes,

99
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:04,480
and safety equipment.

100
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:08,200
Then, something happened that made an already dangerous situation

101
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:09,520
even worse.

102
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:12,620
The station moved into the night side of its orbit.

103
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:14,140
The sunlight disappeared.

104
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:17,040
Luca was now surrounded by darkness.

105
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:21,420
The water across his visor was already making it difficult to see.

106
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:25,220
With the station in orbital night, he could barely make out what was

107
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:26,800
directly in front of him.

108
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:29,860
And then, the water moved over his nose.

109
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:34,240
For the first time, Luca faced the terrifying possibility that his

110
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:37,280
next breath might draw water into his lungs.

111
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:39,940
He needed to get back, quickly.

112
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:41,480
But there was another problem.

113
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:45,440
He was struggling to work out which direction he needed to go.

114
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:49,820
His eyes were almost useless, his communications were unreliable,

115
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:52,760
and he was still outside the station.

116
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:57,740
This was the moment when years of astronaut training became critical.

117
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:02,000
Instead of panicking, Luca began thinking through his options.

118
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:04,220
He remembered his safety tether.

119
00:06:04,340 --> 00:06:07,800
The line that kept him connected to the spacecraft could also help

120
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:09,360
him find his way back.

121
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:11,760
He began using it as a guide,

122
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,560
moving slowly, feeling for handholds, trying to keep his breathing

123
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:20,980
under control, and carefully making his way toward the airlock.

124
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:25,840
At one point, he considered an extraordinary last-resort possibility.

125
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:30,120
If the water overwhelmed him, could he partially vent his spacesuit

126
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:31,600
to help remove it?

127
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,040
But deliberately releasing pressure from a spacesuit would introduce

128
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:36,540
enormous risks.

129
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:39,580
It was an option he hoped never to use.

130
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:43,280
So he continued moving, following the tether,

131
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:47,080
searching for the airlock, and trying to stay calm.

132
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:50,340
Finally, through the water covering his vision,

133
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:53,240
he began to make out part of the airlock entrance.

134
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:54,600
He was close.

135
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:56,600
Luca moved inside.

136
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:59,400
But the danger wasn't completely over.

137
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:02,000
Chris Cassidy still needed to return.

138
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:06,140
The airlock had to be closed, and the chamber had to be pressurized

139
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:09,120
before Luca's helmet could be removed safely.

140
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:13,580
Inside his helmet, the water continued interfering with his hearing.

141
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,860
His communication became increasingly unreliable.

142
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:20,520
At one point, he lost audio contact altogether.

143
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:24,520
So he waited, trying not to move his head too much,

144
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:28,360
hoping the water would not cover his mouth and nose completely.

145
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:31,620
Eventually, the airlock reached a safe pressure.

146
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:33,340
The inner hatch opened.

147
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:35,980
His crewmates moved quickly to help him.

148
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:37,460
They removed his helmet.

149
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:40,980
And at last, Luca was free of the water that had been threatening

150
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:42,040
his breathing.

151
00:07:42,380 --> 00:07:43,840
He had survived.

152
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:47,880
Approximately 100 minutes after the spacewalk began,

153
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:50,120
his helmet was finally removed.

154
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:52,980
But now, NASA faced another question.

155
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:56,900
How could a spacesuit designed to keep an astronaut alive in space

156
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:58,440
fill with water?

157
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:01,380
Engineers began investigating the equipment.

158
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:04,280
They examined the helmet, the ventilation system,

159
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:08,160
and the components responsible for regulating the suit's temperature.

160
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:11,680
And the investigation eventually uncovered the problem.

161
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:15,640
The spacesuit contained a cooling system that circulated water to

162
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:18,160
help prevent the astronaut from overheating.

163
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:22,120
Normally, that water should remain separated from the air used for

164
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:22,920
ventilation.

165
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:27,340
But a component responsible for separating water had become blocked.

166
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:31,820
Investigators found that silica and silicate contamination had obstructed

167
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:34,100
tiny openings in a separator.

168
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:38,060
As a result, cooling water entered the ventilation airflow.

169
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:41,780
And the ventilation system carried that water into Luca's helmet.

170
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:45,720
By the end of the incident, investigators estimated that approximately

171
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:49,220
one and a half liters of water had accumulated there.

172
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:53,000
That's a startling amount of water to have surrounding your head inside

173
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,380
a sealed helmet.

174
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:57,200
But the investigation revealed something else,

175
00:08:57,340 --> 00:09:00,280
something that made the story even more troubling.

176
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:02,860
There had been an earlier warning.

177
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:07,380
During a previous spacewalk, water had also been noticed inside Luca's

178
00:09:07,420 --> 00:09:11,140
helmet. At the time, the crew and ground team believed the problem

179
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:13,680
was probably related to his drinking bag.

180
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,940
So the drinking bag was replaced.

181
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:19,920
The possibility of a more serious equipment failure was not fully

182
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:20,840
understood.

183
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:24,760
Later, investigators determined that neither drinking bag had been

184
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:28,740
leaking. The warning had pointed toward a problem that was much more

185
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:33,780
serious. NASA's investigation identified not only the blocked hardware,

186
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:38,320
but also shortcomings in how unusual equipment behavior had been interpreted.

187
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:42,100
Small amounts of water in spacesuit helmets had sometimes been attributed

188
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:44,140
to sweat or condensation.

189
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:48,180
That familiarity made the new warning easier to underestimate.

190
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:52,500
Engineers and mission teams had not anticipated the full danger posed

191
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:54,300
by this particular failure.

192
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:58,540
The incident led to extensive investigation and corrective actions.

193
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:01,260
NASA examined the contamination problem,

194
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:05,000
improved procedures, and worked to prevent another dangerous water

195
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:09,320
intrusion. But perhaps the most remarkable part of this story is Luca

196
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:14,000
himself. Imagine the physical and mental pressure of that situation.

197
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,100
You're outside the space station.

198
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:17,580
You can barely see.

199
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:20,240
Your hearing and communication are failing.

200
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,680
Your helmet is filling with water.

201
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:25,740
And you have no choice but to stay calm.

202
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:27,920
You need to remember your training.

203
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:31,440
Trust your equipment and find your way home.

204
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,660
Luca did exactly that.

205
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,180
Years of preparation, support from his fellow astronauts,

206
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:41,520
and the response of the ground team helped bring him safely back inside.

207
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:45,800
NASA would later classify the incident as a serious close call.

208
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:50,080
And it serves as a powerful reminder that space exploration is dangerous

209
00:10:50,120 --> 00:10:52,720
in ways most of us never imagine.

210
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:57,740
Sometimes the greatest threat isn't a meteorite or a spacecraft explosion

211
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:00,820
or the endless vacuum surrounding you.

212
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:03,140
Sometimes it's water.

213
00:11:03,420 --> 00:11:08,160
The very thing that makes life possible on Earth nearly took an astronaut's

214
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,880
life hundreds of kilometers above it.

215
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:14,920
This was File Two, The Unknown File.

